div.p:
3. May we not reasonably believe it was by similar appearances that he was manifested in succeeding ages to Enoch, while he ‘walked with God’;
Gen. 5:22, 24. to Noah, before and after the deluge; to Abraham, Isaac, and Jacob on various occasions; and, to mention no more, to Moses. This seems to be the natural meaning of the word: ‘My servant Moses is faithful in all my house. With him will I speak mouth to mouth, even apparently, and not in dark speeches; and the similitude of Jehovah shall he behold’
Cf. Num. 12:7-8.—namely, the Son of God.

5. When he was of due age for executing his priestly office he was manifested to Israel, ‘preaching the gospel of the kingdom of God in every town and in every city’.
Cf. Matt. 4:23; 9:35. And for a time he was glorified by all, who acknowledged that he ‘spake as never man spake’;
Cf. John 7:46. that he ‘spake as one having authority’,
Cf. Matt. 7:29. with all the wisdom of God, and the power of God. He was manifested by numberless ‘signs and wonders, and mighty works which he did’;
Cf. 2 Cor. 12:12. as well as by his whole life, being the only one born of a woman ‘who knew no sin’;
2 Cor. 5:21. who from his birth to his death ‘did all things well’,
Cf. Mark 7:37. doing continually ‘not his own will, but the will of him that sent him’.
Cf. John 6:38.

4. Then error, pain, and all bodily infirmities cease: all these are destroyed by death. And death itself, ‘the last enemy’
1 Cor. 15:26. of man, shall be destroyed at the resurrection. The moment that we hear the voice of the archangel and the trump of God, ‘then shall be fulfilled the saying that is written, Death is swallowed up in victory. This corruptible body shall put on incorruption; this mortal body shall put on immortality;
1 Cor. 15:54. and the Son of God, manifested in the clouds of heaven, shall destroy this last work of the devil.
